nodecode-yandex-mail is a rewrite of akinfold's akinfold/hermes-yandex-mail:

  https://github.com/akinfold/hermes-yandex-mail
  commit f44cf3aff9393d4dc3d420879469d1ebe4735c66

  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Roman Akinfeev (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the eight model-facing tools and their arguments (listing
folders with unread counts, searching, reading a message's text in pages with
its attachment list, saving one attachment to a file, marking read and
flagged, moving, deleting, and sending), the two fences the original carries
(the folder allow-list and the action allow-list, with sending and saving
attachments opt-in so an upgrade never widens what a running agent may do),
the recipient fence, moves that copy before they remove, reading that does
not set the Seen flag, and the whole IMAP and SMTP conversation with verified
TLS. All of it is Common Lisp now: the mail wire, the MIME parser, the
transfer decoders and the address handling are this cell's own, and the
cell talks to the same Yandex IMAP and SMTP servers the plugin did.

What was left out, and why: Hermes' own document cache is not this
organism's, so a saved attachment goes to a directory the settings name
(mail/ under the Nodecode home by default) instead of the harness's cache,
and the saved file's SHA-256 is not reported. The original numbered MIME
parts from the server's BODYSTRUCTURE and fetched one part at a time; this
cell fetches the message once (bounded by the server's own size) and numbers
the parts from its own parse, so a part_id is only meaningful to this cell.
Where a server has no UIDPLUS, an expunge is the folder's, not the
requested UIDs'. A sent message is not filed in the Sent folder, since the
SMTP path and the IMAP path are separate connections here; Yandex files its
own copies. Character sets beyond UTF-8, Windows-1251 and Latin-1 are read as
UTF-8. The Hermes plugin's own packaging (pyproject, plugin.yaml, its test
suite) has no meaning here and is not carried over.
